<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<page xmlns="http://projectmallard.org/1.0/" type="topic" id="projects">
  <info>
    <link type="guide" xref="index#projects"/>
    <link type="seealso" xref="archives"/>
    <title type="sort">1</title>
    <desc>Open, make, rename, copy and remove the projects of a
    workspace.</desc>
  </info>
  <title>Projects</title>
  <p>A project is a lab: its bricks, its events, its disk images and its
  README. Each is a folder of the workspace, with the file
  <file>project.toml</file>, the <file>README.md</file> and the private
  copies of the disks of its machines. Virtualbricks opens the project that
  was open last.</p>
  <section id="window">
    <title>The Projects window</title>
    <p>Choose <guiseq><gui>Projects</gui><gui>Projects…</gui></guiseq>, or
    press <keyseq><key>Ctrl</key><key>O</key></keyseq>. The list has the
    projects of the workspace, the last used first, each with the first line
    of its README, its bricks and when it was used last. A project whose
    images are missing, or whose file can't be read, says so. Typing filters
    the list by name and description. Beside the list, the selected project
    shows its README, its bricks, events and images, and the space that it
    takes.</p>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Open</gui></title>
        <p>Save the open project, and open the selected one.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>New…</gui></title>
        <p>A new, empty project, with a copy of the settings of the open one;
        also <guiseq><gui>Projects</gui><gui>New Project…</gui></guiseq>,
        <keyseq><key>Ctrl</key><key>N</key></keyseq>.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Duplicate…</gui></title>
        <p>A copy of the project, with the private copies of its disks but not
        its images, which the copy shares.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Rename…</gui></title>
        <p>A new name; the dialog says at once if a name can't be used.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Show in Files</gui></title>
        <p>The folder of the project, in the file manager.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Remove…</gui></title>
        <p>The folder of the project goes to the trash, where you can restore
        it, or, where the workspace has no trash, is deleted for good. The
        dialog says how much it takes. The images it uses stay where they are.
        The open project can't be removed: open another one first.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Import…</gui>, <gui>Export…</gui></title>
        <p>A project in one file; see <link xref="archives"/>.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
  </section>
  <section id="save">
    <title>Saving</title>
    <p>Virtualbricks saves the open project every three minutes, when you open
    another one and when it quits. <guiseq><gui>Projects</gui><gui>Save</gui></guiseq>,
    <keyseq><key>Ctrl</key><key>S</key></keyseq>, saves it now.</p>
    <note style="warning">
      <p>Don't edit <file>project.toml</file> while Virtualbricks runs with the
      project open: the next save writes over your changes.</p>
    </note>
  </section>
  <section id="workspace">
    <title>Workspaces</title>
    <p>A workspace is a folder of projects, <file>~/.virtualbricks</file>
    unless the setting <sys>workspace</sys> names another; the
    <gui>Settings</gui> window shows which. It keeps
    the images that its projects share, in <file>shared_images</file>. Started
    with <cmd>virtualbricks --workspace <var>folder</var></cmd>, Virtualbricks
    uses another folder for that run, made if it isn't there, and a
    Virtualbricks can run in each workspace side by side.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="start-up">
    <title>At start</title>
    <p>If the project that was open last can't be opened, the Projects window
    opens and says why. A project written by a newer Virtualbricks is not
    read, and never written over.</p>
  </section>
</page>
